    <div class="bg-secondary/40 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-5 py-4 mb-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Direct Answer</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">A firm high-density seat cushion is the only legitimate way to add seat height to a gas-cylinder office chair. Chair leg extenders do not work — they are made for four-legged furniture and cannot attach to a five-star base on casters. A 6" high-density foam cushion compresses to roughly 3.5"–4" under a 180–220 lb adult; a 3" cushion yields about 1.75"–2". The cost is symmetrical: every inch of cushion subtracts one inch from effective backrest height and pushes the lumbar apex one inch below where the chair set it. Use the thinnest cushion that closes your gap.</p>
    </div>

    <Disclosure />

    <div class="prose-tca">

      <section>
        <h2>Why Most Ergonomic Chairs Top Out Too Low for Tall Users</h2>
        <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
        <p>Premium ergonomic office chairs cap out at 20.5"–21" of seat height — the Herman Miller Aeron Size C at 20.5", the Steelcase Gesture at 21" — while typical popliteal height for a 6'4" adult measures 19.5"–21" barefoot and 20.5"–22" in normal shoes. Mass-market task chairs top out lower still, usually 18"–19". The Steelcase Leap Plus, at a 22.5" maximum, is the only widely available mainstream chair with a seat height ceiling clearing the full 6'0"–6'6" range without a cushion.</p>

        <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. The seat height rule is the least negotiable dimension in seated ergonomics, and it's the one tall users lose most often. Popliteal height is the distance from the floor to the crease behind your knee while seated. If the seat pan sits below that number, your thighs angle upward, your pelvis rotates backward, and the lumbar curve flattens. Every downstream problem — lower back load, femoral compression at the seat edge, feet creeping forward — starts there.</p>

        <p>Here's the arithmetic most chair guides skip. Measure your popliteal height in the shoes you actually wear at your desk, because a standard sneaker or dress shoe heel adds 0.75"–1". Then subtract the chair's published maximum seat height. What remains is your <strong>height gap</strong> — the exact number a cushion has to close.</p>

