        <p><em>Gap figures assume the chair's pneumatic cylinder is already raised to its published maximum. If you have not verified that, do it first — a surprising number of "my chair is too low" complaints are a cylinder sitting two inches below its ceiling.</em></p>

        <p>For the full dimensional picture beyond seat height, see the <a href="/correct-chair-dimensions/" class="link-internal">correct chair dimensions guide</a> and the <a href="/office-chairs-for-6-foot-4/" class="link-internal">6'4" chair fit breakdown</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Chair Leg Extenders Do Not Work on Office Chairs</h2>

        <p>This is the single most common wrong answer to "how do I raise my office chair," and it wastes money and creates a genuine safety problem. Chair leg extenders — also sold as furniture risers, bed risers, or table leg extensions — are cups, sleeves, or threaded inserts that fit over or into a straight vertical furniture leg. They are engineered for dining chairs, desks, tables, and bed frames.</p>

        <p>A gas-cylinder office chair has no legs. The Steelcase Gesture, Steelcase Leap Plus, Herman Miller Aeron, Sihoo Doro S300, and essentially every task chair sold in the last thirty years use the same architecture: a pneumatic cylinder that telescopes vertically through the hub of a five-star base, with a caster pressed into a stem socket at the end of each arm.</p>

        <p>The engineering reason extenders fail is the load path. On a four-legged chair, load runs straight down each leg in compression, so lengthening a leg simply lengthens a compression member. On a five-star base, load enters the hub, travels outward along each arm as a cantilever, and exits into a short caster stem in a press-fit socket. Slipping a riser onto that stem lengthens the moment arm and applies bending to a joint designed only to carry axial load and roll. The base is also the tipping-limit component of the chair, so raising the assembly moves the center of mass up without widening the footprint — the wrong direction for stability.</p>

        <p>Some sellers market "office chair risers" that are really a platform placed under the whole chair. Marginally safer, but the casters now roll on a small elevated surface with an edge, so normal repositioning walks a caster over the lip. Not a solution for a chair you move during the day.</p>

        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm">
          <strong>Note on testing:</strong> I have personally used and measured only one chair — the Steelcase Gesture in my apartment. Every cushion assessment on this page is based on published product dimensions, foam density specifications, and engineering analysis of seated load geometry. No cushion on this page has been personally tested.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How Much Height Does a Seat Cushion Actually Add?</h2>

        <p>Less than the box says. Foam thickness is quoted unloaded, and the number that matters is the loaded thickness under your body weight. As a working rule from published density and compression data, a high-density polyurethane foam cushion retains roughly 55–70% of its stated thickness under a 180–220 lb adult, concentrated at the ischial tuberosities where nearly all seated load lands.</p>

