        <h3>Why memory foam is the wrong foam for height</h3>
        <p>Memory foam is viscoelastic: it is designed to yield progressively under sustained load and conform to body contour. That behavior is excellent for pressure redistribution and terrible for holding a seated hip point at a fixed elevation. A 4" memory foam cushion can settle another half inch over a two-hour work session, which means your seat height drifts downward through the day. High-density non-viscoelastic foam, or a firm foam core with a thin comfort top layer, holds its loaded height far more predictably.</p>

        <h3>Verify the number yourself</h3>
        <p>Sit on the cushion normally and have someone measure from the floor to its top surface beside your thigh, about 4" back from the front edge. Compare against the same measurement without it. That difference is your real lift.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Trade-Off Nobody Mentions: Cushion Height vs. Backrest and Lumbar Position</h2>

        <p>This is where most seat-cushion advice falls apart, and it matters more for tall users than anyone else. A cushion does not raise the chair. It raises <em>you</em> inside the chair. The seat pan, the backrest, the lumbar mechanism, and the armrests all stay exactly where they were.</p>

        <p>Backrest height on ergonomic chairs is measured from the seat pan: 24" on the Steelcase Gesture, roughly 23.5" on the Herman Miller Aeron Size C, 25.5" on the Steelcase Leap Plus. A 3" loaded cushion turns the Gesture into a 21" backrest and the Aeron Size C into a 20.5" backrest. For a 6'4" user whose shoulder blades already sit near the top edge of a 24" back, losing three inches means the backrest now ends mid-thoracic — precisely the support tall users are shortest on to begin with.</p>

        <p>The lumbar problem is sharper. For a 6'4" seated adult, the L3–L4 apex of the lumbar curve sits roughly 8"–10" above the seat pan. Ergonomic chairs typically provide only 3"–4" of lumbar height adjustment. Add a 3"–4" cushion and your lumbar apex moves above the top of that adjustment range entirely — the support is now pressing into your sacrum rather than your lumbar curve. That is functionally worse than no lumbar support, because it drives the pelvis into the posterior rotation the chair was bought to prevent.</p>

        <p>Armrests carry the same penalty. Armrest height is specified relative to the seat pan, so a 3" cushion effectively removes 3" from the top of the armrest range. Tall users are already at the top of that range — the <a href="/review/gesture/" class="link-internal">Gesture's 4D armrests</a> are one of the few that reach far enough at 6'4". A thick cushion gives that back.</p>

        <p><strong>The design rule this produces:</strong> max out the pneumatic cylinder first, then close the remaining gap with the thinnest firm cushion that reaches it. Never buy a cushion thicker than your measured gap. A 6" cushion bought to solve a 1" problem costs you four inches of backrest, four inches of armrest range, and puts your lumbar curve out of reach of the mechanism — a net ergonomic loss on a chair that was otherwise nearly right.</p>

        <h3>Match cushion thickness to your gap</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Gap of 1" or less:</strong> A 2" high-density cushion (~1.3" loaded). Backrest cost is negligible; lumbar stays within adjustment range. This is the common case at 6'2"–6'4" on a Gesture or Aeron Size C.</li>
          <li><strong>Gap of 1"–2":</strong> A 3" high-density cushion (~2" loaded). Raise the lumbar mechanism toward its maximum before you add the cushion, so the net position lands near correct. Expect to lower your monitor plan and re-check desk clearance.</li>
          <li><strong>Gap above 2":</strong> Do not solve this with foam. Change the chair or change the cylinder. A 4"–6" cushion converts an ergonomic chair into a padded stool with a low back.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Second-order effects on the rest of the workstation</h3>
        <p>Raising your hip point by 2"–4" moves everything else. Your elbow rises relative to a fixed-height desk, which pushes you toward wrist extension and shoulder depression unless the desk goes up too — see the <a href="/standing-desk-height-tall-people/" class="link-internal">standing desk height guide</a> for the elbow-height formula. Your eye line rises by the same amount, so a monitor already positioned correctly is now low; the <a href="/monitor-arm-tall-people/" class="link-internal">monitor arm height guide</a> covers the reach specs that matter. And if the cushion lifts you above your popliteal height, your feet lose solid floor contact and you'll need a footrest. Check the thigh-clearance side of the equation in the <a href="/knee-pain-seat-depth/" class="link-internal">seat depth and knee pressure guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Seat Cushions Worth Considering (and an Honest Caveat)</h2>

        <p>Be clear-eyed about this category. Almost no seat-riser cushion sold on Amazon is purpose-built for tall users. These are general-purpose foam cushions — designed for comfort, coccyx relief, or car seats — that sellers reposition with height-boost keywords. There is no tall-specific engineering behind them, no published compression data at load, and listing dimensions are often approximate. Buy accordingly: choose on foam type, thickness, and return policy, not on marketing claims.</p>

        <h3>MABOZOO Extra Thick Chair Cushion (18" × 16" × 6")</h3>
        <p><strong>Stated thickness:</strong> 6" &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Estimated loaded lift:</strong> ~3.5"–4" &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Footprint:</strong> 18" × 16" &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Typical price:</strong> $35–$45</p>
        <p>This is the option for a large gap — a 6'6" user on a mass-market task chair, a fixed-height gaming or conference chair, or any seat with a hard ceiling well below popliteal height. The 6" build is the thickest commonly available in a firm foam construction, and the 18" × 16" footprint fits most ergonomic seat pans without overhanging the front edge, which matters because an overhanging cushion reintroduces the popliteal pressure you were trying to avoid.</p>
        <p>Understand what you are trading. At roughly 4" of loaded lift, this cushion removes about four inches of effective backrest and moves your lumbar curve above nearly any chair's adjustment range. It is the right answer when the chair has no meaningful lumbar system to lose. It is the wrong answer on a Gesture, Aeron, or Leap where you would be degrading a mechanism you paid for.</p>

